---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Chrome from "../components/Chrome.astro";
import { DECK_NAME, DECK_VERSION, PHYSICAL_DECK_URL } from "../config";

const description =
  "What Grasping Straws is, where it comes from, and why the physical deck is the real thing.";
---

<Base title={`About — ${DECK_NAME}`} description={description} bodyClass="about">
  <Chrome />
  <main id="main">
    <span class="glyph page-mark"></span>

    <h1>{DECK_NAME}</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      When you’re stuck, reaching for a random card feels like grasping straws. But the drowning
      man’s error isn’t grabbing &mdash; it’s assuming everything within reach is a straw.
      Sometimes you close your hand around a diamond. The question mark is the whole method: you
      won’t know which you drew until you use it.
    </p>

    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      Grasping Straws is an original deck of lateral-thinking prompts for breaking creative
      deadlocks &mdash; in music, film, writing, or anything else. Each card is an instruction, a
      question, or a koan. Draw one, take it seriously for ten minutes, and see where it puts your
      attention.
    </p>

    <h2>Two editions</h2>
    <p>
      There are two decks. The Paper edition works on the piece in front of you. The{" "}
      <a href="/night/">Night edition</a> works on the person making it, for the days when the
      piece is not the problem. Each has fifty-two cards, and no card is in both.
    </p>

    <h2>Lineage</h2>
    <p>
      {/* The space before the link must be explicit: the HTML minifier
          collapses a newline between text and an inline element to nothing,
          which rendered as "inspired byOblique Strategies". */}
      The deck is inspired by{" "}
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oblique_Strategies" rel="noopener">Oblique Strategies</a>,
      the card deck Brian Eno and Peter Schmidt first published in 1975. The debt is to the form,
      not the words: every card here is original.
    </p>

    <h2 id="physical">Why the physical deck</h2>
    <p>
      This site deals cards with algorithmic randomness &mdash; a formula pretending to be chance.
      A paper deck shuffled by your own hands is the real thing: true randomness, and the small
      ritual of cutting the deck and turning a card over. The screen version is a sample; the
      object is the instrument.
    </p>
    {
      PHYSICAL_DECK_URL ? (
        <p>
          <a href={PHYSICAL_DECK_URL} rel="noopener">Get the physical deck</a>
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          The physical deck is coming soon: fifty-two prompts, a title card and an instructions
          card, poker-size, printed to order. The day it exists, every link to it on this site
          points there.
        </p>
      )
    }
    <p>
      Until then there is a free <a href="/print-and-play.pdf">print-and-play edition</a> &mdash;
      the whole Paper deck and the five rules cards, nine to a page with cut lines. Paper you cut
      yourself is still paper.
    </p>

    {/* The one link off the site that is not a source: who made this, and
        where the rest of his work is. */}
    <p class="credit">
      Deck v{DECK_VERSION} &middot; <a href="/today/">Today’s card</a> &middot;
      <a href="/play/">Ways to play</a> &middot; <a href="/deck/">The deck</a> &middot; Made by{" "}
      <a href="https://lscaturchio.xyz" rel="noopener">Lorenzo Scaturchio</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
